  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-ETH152</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced capacity of constituents and stakeholders to comply with international labour standards and address unacceptable forms of work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The development strategy of Ethiopia has been articulated in the Ten-Year Perspective Plan. The garment and textile industries are among the priorities in the move of lifting Ethiopia to a middle-income country. According to the Plan of Action for Job Creation 2020- 2025, employment in the textiles and apparel industry is estimated at 798,752 in 2018 and is forecast to grow 86% through 2025, creating more that 683,000 new direct jobs and almost 868,000 new indirect jobs. 
However, lack of compliance with ratified convention and labour law, poor working conditions and low wages resulting in low productivity, high turnover and absentee rates; as well as weak regulatory institutions, coupled with the limited capacity of the workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations, hinder the proper functioning of the labour market and businesses environment. 
In order to improve the sector performance, the ILO implements a coordinated programme under the CPO ETH152 aimed at facilitating dialogue among multiple stakeholders to develop a common vision and strategies to make Ethiopia an African hub of socially responsible production of garment for both global and domestic market. The programme supports also tripartite constituents in fixing a minimum wage as well as in enhancing the capacity of government institutions to prevent and resolve labour disputes. 
At the regional and sectoral level, the programme will focus on strengthening employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organization capacity for social dialogue and collective barraging. It also assists the labour inspectorate in building the capacity of its inspectors. To ensure occupational health and safety, the programme aims to establish a sustainable workplace injury prevention, protection and compensation system in collaboration with social security agencies and other relevant partners. 
At the factory level, the programme aims to establish a robust, sustainable and inclusive compliance system.</narrative>
  </description>
